<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Western Zhou</h1><p class="st-meta">440 BCE – 256 BCE (approx.) · capital Henan (Wangcheng)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The tiny duchy around Luoyang that sheltered the last Zhou kings and surrendered to Qin in 256 BCE, ending the Zhou royal line.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 440 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-kao-of-zhou">King Kao of Zhou</a> enfeoffed his younger brother Jie, Duke Huan, at Henan (the old royal city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangcheng">Wangcheng</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>). The duchy took over much of what remained of the shrunken royal domain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou</a> were the real masters of the land around the king, who by the 4th century BCE had almost no territory of his own. After a succession dispute in 367 BCE the domain split, with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-eastern-zhou">Duchy of Eastern Zhou</a> ruling around Gong. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-nan-of-zhou">King Nan</a> lived under the protection of Western Zhou, and the duchy survived by playing off the great powers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 256 BCE Duke Wu of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou</a> joined an alliance against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> attacked, the duke surrendered his 36 towns, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-nan-of-zhou">King Nan</a> died that year. Qin removed the Nine Tripods, symbols of royal legitimacy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> came to an end.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 440 BCE – 256 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:50.95%;width:3.66%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>440 BCE – 256 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Henan (Wangcheng)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duke Huan of Western Zhou, Duke Wu of Western Zhou</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Western Zhou (Warring States)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=duchy-of-western-zhou" aria-label="Duchy of Western Zhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7574 949 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Western Zhou lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7894 1205L7855 1179M7894 1205L7768 1187"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M7768 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7894 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7894 1205h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Henan. <a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=duchy-of-western-zhou">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

